WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.169
It's great to welcome back to the program
today.

00:00:02.169 --> 00:00:08.780
Congressman Roe Karna representing California's
17th District, located in the heart of Silicon

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:10.830
Valley.

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:11.830
Where to start?

00:00:11.830 --> 00:00:12.830
Right.

00:00:12.830 --> 00:00:17.800
I mean, I think maybe the first place would
be second stimulus, given what's going on

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:26.690
with coronavirus discussions of a payroll
tax, discussions of an extension or expansion.

00:00:26.690 --> 00:00:30.380
And to what degree of this enhanced unemployment?

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:31.630
What are your priorities?

00:00:31.630 --> 00:00:33.860
What do you think needs to happen and when?

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:37.000
Well, first, we have to be clear eyed about
what the problem is.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:38.930
The problem is that there are not enough jobs.

00:00:38.930 --> 00:00:44.740
I mean, people don't have enough money to
pay basic rent to get groceries.

00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:48.180
So when people say, oh, these unemployment
checks are some disincentive to work, they're

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:52.329
five times the job seekers as there are jobs.

00:00:52.329 --> 00:00:58.089
And the irony is that by not giving this money,
it's not just that we're doing enormous damage

00:00:58.089 --> 00:00:59.910
to middle class and working class families.

00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:02.239
I mean, some are having foreclosures.

00:01:02.239 --> 00:01:05.750
Some aren't being able to get enough nutritious
food for their kids.

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:09.350
It's also that we're damaging the economy
because the economy is stalling.

00:01:09.350 --> 00:01:11.410
If people don't have money to spend.

00:01:11.410 --> 00:01:16.680
So the priority has to be to stick to the
six hundred dollars to get that extended through

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:20.650
the end of the year to get funding for states
and localities.

00:01:20.650 --> 00:01:25.270
Of course, as you know, David, I've been pushing
for two thousand dollars a month for for people

00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:26.980
that wasn't in the House bill.

00:01:26.980 --> 00:01:29.990
So I don't want to mislead people and it's
unrealistic that's going to be in the cards

00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:30.990
for this.

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:35.600
But but we have to stick to the six hundred
dollars, the stimulus and the funny money

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:37.880
for state and local governments.

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:43.550
I was initially at least sort of loosely open
to the idea of the payroll tax cut that has

00:01:43.550 --> 00:01:45.400
been floated by Donald Trump.

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:51.170
I then changed my mind because I realize that
it seems like a way to weaken Social Security

00:01:51.170 --> 00:01:56.710
and Medicare and then maybe use that deliberate
weakening as a reason to justify further weakening

00:01:56.710 --> 00:01:57.710
or defunding.

00:01:57.710 --> 00:01:59.320
So I ended up deciding I didn't like it.

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:04.060
And in particular because the way I read it,
it's only a holiday.

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:05.940
It is not actually a cut.

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:11.349
In other words, you would then on your taxes
when you file, potentially owe it back.

00:02:11.349 --> 00:02:14.060
And so I ended up saying, I don't like it.

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:15.239
Where are you on it?

00:02:15.239 --> 00:02:20.189
Yeah, I don't think it makes sense, particularly
because we have the challenge now of businesses

00:02:20.189 --> 00:02:21.189
not opening.

00:02:21.189 --> 00:02:24.890
I mean, if you if you work for a restaurant
or indoor dining, it doesn't matter if you

00:02:24.890 --> 00:02:26.549
have a payroll tax cut.

00:02:26.549 --> 00:02:30.159
That's not going to get you back to work until
the virus is under control.

00:02:30.159 --> 00:02:35.569
And I don't see it getting under control until
next year, until we have a vaccine and better

00:02:35.569 --> 00:02:36.569
therapeutics.

00:02:36.569 --> 00:02:43.459
So you could argue in a traditional recession,
a payroll tax cut may provide some added incentive

00:02:43.459 --> 00:02:45.700
to the hiring.

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:47.900
It favors businesses over workers.

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:50.780
But you may say, OK, we need it as one tool.

00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:56.640
But in a pandemic where the ability to create
new jobs is restricted by public health, I

00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:58.480
don't think it makes sense.

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:05.700
So I'm curious whether you have any other
as a member of the House, insight into what

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:08.829
we might call the real case numbers.

00:03:08.829 --> 00:03:14.750
And what I mean by that is a couple of examples
are we saw this leveling off after July 16th,

00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:19.529
which was exactly when data started going
to HHS instead of the CDC.

00:03:19.529 --> 00:03:24.670
It's a question mark, although I'm not alleging
deliberate foul play yet.

00:03:24.670 --> 00:03:28.169
But then on top of that, Florida's numbers
have gone down.

00:03:28.169 --> 00:03:32.419
But they stopped testing on Thursday because
of the hurricane and only restarted yesterday.

00:03:32.419 --> 00:03:36.360
So that would be a very clear reason why we
don't have a complete picture there.

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:41.280
The California covered 19 dashboard yesterday
put up a message saying that for several days,

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:45.579
cases had been underreported, although the
reason why is not yet clear to me.

00:03:45.579 --> 00:03:48.249
Are you working with the same data the public
is working with?

00:03:48.249 --> 00:03:52.499
You have any other insights into into more
accurate data?

00:03:52.499 --> 00:03:53.499
We're working with the same data.

00:03:53.499 --> 00:03:56.450
We don't get any briefing of better data.

00:03:56.450 --> 00:04:00.249
And I say whether it's deliberate, I don't
know.

00:04:00.249 --> 00:04:01.620
But it's certainly not transparent.

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:04.060
I mean, there's been a lot of obfuscation.

00:04:04.060 --> 00:04:07.859
There isn't a clear way of collecting this
data.

00:04:07.859 --> 00:04:14.599
And the reality is it's the hospitalizations
and the deaths that continue to be outrageous.

00:04:14.599 --> 00:04:19.880
I mean, especially when you look at other
countries, the cases, of course, matter.

00:04:19.880 --> 00:04:21.569
But the No.

00:04:21.569 --> 00:04:26.650
One is can dispute the data that we still
don't have this under control, given the amount

00:04:26.650 --> 00:04:30.370
of deaths that we're unfortunately facing
and given the amount of hospitalization we're

00:04:30.370 --> 00:04:31.530
facing.

00:04:31.530 --> 00:04:38.611
The topic of cases, cases versus tests, if
you don't test, you don't find cases, case

00:04:38.611 --> 00:04:41.390
fatality rate versus deaths per capita.

00:04:41.390 --> 00:04:45.290
This all kind of exploded on Monday night
in this interview that the president gave

00:04:45.290 --> 00:04:46.510
to Axios.

00:04:46.510 --> 00:04:49.530
Jonathan Swann, I'm guessing you saw the interview.

00:04:49.530 --> 00:04:50.530
I did.

00:04:50.530 --> 00:04:52.860
I mean, and, you know, it's very hard.

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:54.240
The president has one skill.

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:55.940
He's he's very good at marketing and branding.

00:04:55.940 --> 00:04:57.520
But you can't spin death.

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:00.440
You can't market when people are in hospitals
or film.

00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:05.680
And so he was trying to say when the relevant
statistic is the percentage of people with

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:09.000
cases who are dying.

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:14.639
And it is true that that percentage has gone
down as treatments have improved.

00:05:14.639 --> 00:05:16.940
But that is not the relevant statistic.

00:05:16.940 --> 00:05:22.409
A relevant statistic is not what percentage
of people are getting covered or dying.

00:05:22.409 --> 00:05:26.680
The relevant statistic is why are so many
Americans getting covered compared to other

00:05:26.680 --> 00:05:27.720
countries?

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:33.050
And I think the president was unable to spin
his way out of that.

00:05:33.050 --> 00:05:34.259
He obviously had.

00:05:34.259 --> 00:05:36.940
Stands the distinction, he's just hoping that
he can fool people.

00:05:36.940 --> 00:05:40.039
But you can't fool people when it's their
own public health.

00:05:40.039 --> 00:05:41.039
That's interesting.

00:05:41.039 --> 00:05:46.860
So you think he does understand these these
issues in more detail than maybe he lets on

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:50.970
and he's spinning because I actually question
whether he understands some of this stuff.

00:05:50.970 --> 00:05:54.539
Your sense is that it's deliberate and that
it's optics.

00:05:54.539 --> 00:05:58.379
I think in that case, it's I mean, I don't
think he's deeply reflective, but I think

00:05:58.379 --> 00:06:03.380
he looks for the numbers that make the most
sense and he tries that to put them out.

00:06:03.380 --> 00:06:06.580
If if the death rates were lower, he would
have put that number out.

00:06:06.580 --> 00:06:13.419
So I. I think his modus operandi his whole
life has been take any news, exaggerated a

00:06:13.419 --> 00:06:15.280
bit, make it sound good.

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:16.280
Great.

00:06:16.280 --> 00:06:17.280
And to your advantage.

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:22.000
And in many cases that has work with him,
with people who are busy in their lives and

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:25.810
don't have the time to look through long articles.

00:06:25.810 --> 00:06:31.639
The challenge here is suddenly people are
taking the time to pay attention because it's

00:06:31.639 --> 00:06:32.639
their own house.

00:06:32.639 --> 00:06:33.639
It's their parents' house.

00:06:33.639 --> 00:06:35.030
It's their neighbor's house.

00:06:35.030 --> 00:06:36.379
And so they have far more details.

00:06:36.379 --> 00:06:41.509
And when you have a situation of an engaged
citizenry on an issue, Trump strategy really

00:06:41.509 --> 00:06:42.600
falls flat.

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:46.729
And that's why I think you've seen him struggling.

00:06:46.729 --> 00:06:48.199
What do you think?

00:06:48.199 --> 00:06:50.379
I guess let me rephrase it in a different
way.

00:06:50.379 --> 00:06:58.500
I imagine that if you were scheduled to do
an interview that your staff felt very strongly

00:06:58.500 --> 00:07:04.379
would potentially be a disaster, that you
logic would reach you and you might listen

00:07:04.379 --> 00:07:08.940
to advice that says this maybe isn't a good
interview to do.

00:07:08.940 --> 00:07:15.139
The context is, how do you think these insane
interviews are even happening with the president?

00:07:15.139 --> 00:07:19.889
Is it that he doesn't listen to his staff
and that he believes he'll always come out

00:07:19.889 --> 00:07:20.889
on top?

00:07:20.889 --> 00:07:23.370
I mean, I'm trying to understand how these
things are happening so frequently.

00:07:23.370 --> 00:07:24.370
Yeah.

00:07:24.370 --> 00:07:27.509
I think from his perspective, he's succeeded.

00:07:27.509 --> 00:07:31.460
Quote unquote, succeeded in a material sense
that a lot of things he's done in life, he's

00:07:31.460 --> 00:07:35.849
managed to talk himself out of bankruptcy
as he came out of nowhere and became president

00:07:35.849 --> 00:07:36.849
of the United States.

00:07:36.849 --> 00:07:42.819
And I think he has a lot of self-confidence
and hubris that his view is the more exposure,

00:07:42.819 --> 00:07:46.439
the better, and that he can talk his way out
of anything.

00:07:46.439 --> 00:07:51.090
And this is a common problem when people become
president.

00:07:51.090 --> 00:07:59.120
Obviously, it's massively exaggerated in Trump's
case, where he does not have the humility

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:00.909
to see his own weaknesses.

00:08:00.909 --> 00:08:04.650
And I think that's creating a large amount
of the problems for him.

00:08:04.650 --> 00:08:10.909
So what what does that get you thinking about
in terms of potential Biden vs. Trump debates?

00:08:10.909 --> 00:08:16.120
I've said from a practical standpoint, I can't
think of an election I voted in where I care

00:08:16.120 --> 00:08:17.710
less about seeing debates.

00:08:17.710 --> 00:08:22.560
I mean, it's just I just think I have more
than enough information, uniquely so in this

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:23.639
case, to make a decision.

00:08:23.639 --> 00:08:25.699
And that's that's to vote for Joe Biden.

00:08:25.699 --> 00:08:28.639
There's speculation about Joe Biden.

00:08:28.639 --> 00:08:31.819
The debates are better for Biden or the debates
are better for Trump.

00:08:31.819 --> 00:08:35.969
And a lot of that depends on, you know, what
your belief is about the cognitive state of

00:08:35.969 --> 00:08:37.469
either or both of them.

00:08:37.469 --> 00:08:39.030
What's your sense about the debates?

00:08:39.030 --> 00:08:41.529
Not generally, but in this particular case?

00:08:41.529 --> 00:08:47.810
Well, I think that Biden will debate and should
debate because I think the story of him not

00:08:47.810 --> 00:08:53.390
debating would be a much bigger deal and lead
to speculation that is unfair and wrong.

00:08:53.390 --> 00:08:54.980
And I think people underestimate Biden.

00:08:54.980 --> 00:08:59.530
I mean, if you look at you know, I co-chair
of the Bernie Sanders campaign, we were very

00:08:59.530 --> 00:09:02.700
excited when we had a one on one opportunity
with Biden.

00:09:02.700 --> 00:09:05.310
And I think everyone will tell you that Biden
held his own.

00:09:05.310 --> 00:09:09.500
I mean, it was not a knockout punch for Bernie
Sanders of anything.

00:09:09.500 --> 00:09:11.079
I think it was probably a draw.

00:09:11.079 --> 00:09:17.440
So my sense is Biden's debate skills improved
a lot through the course of the primary.

00:09:17.440 --> 00:09:23.139
And I think one of the mistakes Donald Trump
is making is by so putting all his chips in

00:09:23.139 --> 00:09:31.200
these debates and setting the bar so low,
if Biden has a satisfactory performance, is

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:33.561
going to really, I think, get the upper hand.

00:09:33.561 --> 00:09:38.180
And I think Biden's tape perfectly capable
of having a solid performance.

00:09:38.180 --> 00:09:43.810
That's super interesting, because I remember
that in the sort of Twitter Bernie space before

00:09:43.810 --> 00:09:48.061
the one on one debates, there were very high
hopes that Joe Biden was going to implode.

00:09:48.061 --> 00:09:49.380
And this was God.

00:09:49.380 --> 00:09:51.850
That was going to be the moment at which Bernie
took over.

00:09:51.850 --> 00:09:54.790
And as you mention, it really was a draw in
a sense.

00:09:54.790 --> 00:09:59.120
I do think that the heavy focus on Corona
virus probably helped Joe Biden to some degree.

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:02.300
And there was less focus in that debate on
on other issues.

00:10:02.300 --> 00:10:07.330
But so even within the Bernie camp, there
were high hopes about that one on one debate.

00:10:07.330 --> 00:10:11.269
Yeah, no, I mean, that was sort of our final
hope to try to shake up the race.

00:10:11.269 --> 00:10:13.829
Obviously, we were doing very well headed
into Super Tuesday.

00:10:13.829 --> 00:10:17.339
We had a different disappointing Super Tuesday.

00:10:17.339 --> 00:10:21.720
It was a not anticipated that everyone was
withdrawn and endorsed by the.

00:10:21.720 --> 00:10:22.720
It's a free country.

00:10:22.720 --> 00:10:23.720
People can do that.

00:10:23.720 --> 00:10:27.730
But really, no one thought the establishment
would coalesce around by them that quickly.

00:10:27.730 --> 00:10:32.940
And so we needed a game changer and we thought
this debate was a place we could have won.

00:10:32.940 --> 00:10:33.940
I mean, Bernie.

00:10:33.940 --> 00:10:38.139
Actively, I think, and done much better in
a lot of the debates leading up to that debate

00:10:38.139 --> 00:10:39.899
than Biden had.

00:10:39.899 --> 00:10:43.940
Biden is exceeded expectations in that debate.

00:10:43.940 --> 00:10:49.110
And so he's certainly capable of doing that.

00:10:49.110 --> 00:10:56.820
And so I think Trump is making a mistake if
he's putting all his eggs in in that basket.

00:10:56.820 --> 00:11:01.680
So let's talk a little bit about the last
three months here to the election where every

00:11:01.680 --> 00:11:07.800
member of the House is up for reelection,
33 plus two Senate seats are available.

00:11:07.800 --> 00:11:10.370
And, of course, it's the presidential election.

00:11:10.370 --> 00:11:16.210
There are some in my audience that are feeling
very confident about the presidential piece,

00:11:16.210 --> 00:11:20.360
seeing polls that have Joe Biden doing very
well today.

00:11:20.360 --> 00:11:25.220
I went through and pointed out, for example,
in a recent poll where Joe Biden is winning

00:11:25.220 --> 00:11:26.220
by nine.

00:11:26.220 --> 00:11:31.800
When you look at the margin of error, that
could be actually only a two point four percent

00:11:31.800 --> 00:11:33.430
lead for Joe Biden.

00:11:33.430 --> 00:11:39.920
And Hillary won by two point two in 2016 at
the popular vote level, but lost the Electoral

00:11:39.920 --> 00:11:40.959
College.

00:11:40.959 --> 00:11:46.671
When I look at state by state polling, which
looks quite good for Joe Biden, the state

00:11:46.671 --> 00:11:49.420
polling was not great in 2016.

00:11:49.420 --> 00:11:50.430
National was good.

00:11:50.430 --> 00:11:53.350
In other words, Hillary was up three and one
by two point two.

00:11:53.350 --> 00:11:54.700
That's that's great.

00:11:54.700 --> 00:11:57.660
Wisconsin said Hillary was going to win and
she lost.

00:11:57.660 --> 00:12:04.579
So I am very cautious about any complacency
at all going into this election.

00:12:04.579 --> 00:12:07.120
While some are extraordinarily confident.

00:12:07.120 --> 00:12:10.949
What do you think is the right perspective
on the data?

00:12:10.949 --> 00:12:12.420
I think caution is better.

00:12:12.420 --> 00:12:18.180
I would say if the election were tomorrow,
Biden would win because I think the the polling

00:12:18.180 --> 00:12:20.060
is so overwhelming in one direction.

00:12:20.060 --> 00:12:24.279
But the election is not tomorrow and there's
a lot that can happen in the next 90 days.

00:12:24.279 --> 00:12:25.480
The debates being one of them.

00:12:25.480 --> 00:12:29.750
That doesn't mean don't debate because that
would be an issue in and of itself.

00:12:29.750 --> 00:12:31.430
But the point is that the conventions haven't
happened.

00:12:31.430 --> 00:12:33.120
The debates haven't happened.

00:12:33.120 --> 00:12:37.100
We don't know what the economy is going to
look like.

00:12:37.100 --> 00:12:38.579
Come end of September and October.

00:12:38.579 --> 00:12:42.010
We don't know whether coronaviruses is going
to be on the retreat.

00:12:42.010 --> 00:12:46.380
In some sense and getting slightly better
or whether it's getting worse.

00:12:46.380 --> 00:12:49.680
We don't know the impact of who he picks of
the vice president.

00:12:49.680 --> 00:12:55.360
So this is not a situation of George W. Bush
in 2008 where George W. Bush collapsed into

00:12:55.360 --> 00:13:00.420
the 30s and really the American people had
totally written him off.

00:13:00.420 --> 00:13:04.600
And I don't think it's even at the level of
Jimmy Carter Rubin been I really admired Jimmy

00:13:04.600 --> 00:13:05.600
Carter.

00:13:05.600 --> 00:13:07.990
I think he was an exceptional person.

00:13:07.990 --> 00:13:11.180
But Trump has been stubbornly at 40, 41, 42.

00:13:11.180 --> 00:13:16.410
That means if he gets a five point swing and
gets his approval up to forty five, this is

00:13:16.410 --> 00:13:17.790
a very competitive race.

00:13:17.790 --> 00:13:24.180
And then he put it, if you put on top of that
this fact that the voter suppression.

00:13:24.180 --> 00:13:30.690
The difficulty of mail in ballots, the energetic
nature of his base.

00:13:30.690 --> 00:13:35.170
And I think we have to assume that this is
going to be a drag out fight.

00:13:35.170 --> 00:13:41.139
When it comes to the Senate, it looks to me
as though if Democrats keep the House, which

00:13:41.139 --> 00:13:46.699
you are projected to, if Joe Biden were to
win, that a lot of what gets done in the next

00:13:46.699 --> 00:13:51.360
two years depends on whether Democrats can
take control of the Senate.

00:13:51.360 --> 00:13:55.019
And I've looked at the races up and down,
and certainly there's a path now that is a

00:13:55.019 --> 00:14:00.000
clearer path than than it was six and 12 and
18 months ago.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:02.570
What races are you looking at in the Senate?

00:14:02.570 --> 00:14:06.010
And what's your sense of where that might
go?

00:14:06.010 --> 00:14:09.560
I think Arizona, we've got a great job with
Mark Kelly.

00:14:09.560 --> 00:14:14.339
I think in North Carolina with Cunningham,
we have a very, very strong shot.

00:14:14.339 --> 00:14:16.389
I think Susan Collins is in deep trouble.

00:14:16.389 --> 00:14:21.949
I think Steve Bullock in Montana has made
that race much more competitive.

00:14:21.949 --> 00:14:23.120
Our candidates are doing very well.

00:14:23.120 --> 00:14:25.740
But I think it largely depends on on Biden.

00:14:25.740 --> 00:14:29.860
I mean, if if Biden has a kind of win that
he is polling currently shows, I think we

00:14:29.860 --> 00:14:32.529
have a very good chance of carrying the Senate.

00:14:32.529 --> 00:14:40.019
If this race tightens, as I fear it may, then,
you know, our chances of getting a sweep we

00:14:40.019 --> 00:14:45.589
need in Senate seats becomes harder and it
became harder where some of the candidates

00:14:45.589 --> 00:14:49.870
won the right candidates from the Republican
side in the Kansas primary.

00:14:49.870 --> 00:14:53.519
And, you know, they have and they've been
fortunate not to elect some of the most crazy

00:14:53.519 --> 00:14:57.230
people where we could have had a pickup opportunity.

00:14:57.230 --> 00:15:03.500
Last thing I want to touch on, there's been
this idea now of could an election be delayed?

00:15:03.500 --> 00:15:04.889
What would it look like?

00:15:04.889 --> 00:15:06.690
What would be the reasons?

00:15:06.690 --> 00:15:09.560
Are there ways for the president to stay in
the Oval Office?

00:15:09.560 --> 00:15:12.860
And I've tried not to give this more attention
than it deserves, because when when it comes

00:15:12.860 --> 00:15:16.990
right down to it, we've not had a federal
election delay in the history of this country.

00:15:16.990 --> 00:15:18.339
On January 20th.

00:15:18.339 --> 00:15:20.620
Trump stops being president if he's not reelected.

00:15:20.620 --> 00:15:23.199
And then, you know, a series of other things
happen.

00:15:23.199 --> 00:15:25.880
Is this at all being taken seriously among
you and your colleagues?

00:15:25.880 --> 00:15:29.610
Is there is there discussion of this or is
it basically just not not being paid attention

00:15:29.610 --> 00:15:30.610
to?

00:15:30.610 --> 00:15:32.839
My concern is not that he's going to delay
the election.

00:15:32.839 --> 00:15:40.459
My concern is that there is a early reporting
of Election Day results where which tend to

00:15:40.459 --> 00:15:42.060
favor Republicans traditionally.

00:15:42.060 --> 00:15:47.410
And he may be have an early lead and well
in one of these battleground states.

00:15:47.410 --> 00:15:51.209
And then there's delay and not proper counting
of absentee ballots.

00:15:51.209 --> 00:15:56.350
And if you have a few percent election in
a place like Florida in a place like Wisconsin,

00:15:56.350 --> 00:16:02.339
that there's tremendous pressure put on the
state legislatures to certify those states

00:16:02.339 --> 00:16:03.800
in favor of Trump.

00:16:03.800 --> 00:16:09.730
So I don't see a situation where the state
certified Joe Biden in the House and Senate

00:16:09.730 --> 00:16:12.850
certified Joe Biden and Donald Trump stays
in the White House.

00:16:12.850 --> 00:16:20.379
I think a far more risky situation is like
1876, where you actually have state legislatures

00:16:20.379 --> 00:16:28.810
working, conspiring with the White House to
give the result to Trump on on a on a close

00:16:28.810 --> 00:16:29.810
call.

00:16:29.810 --> 00:16:32.759
And that that to me is the the disenfranchisement.

00:16:32.759 --> 00:16:37.720
And states calling it for Trump are what what
I think are the deep concerns.

00:16:37.720 --> 00:16:42.879
Yeah, I'm not conspiratorial by nature, but
after months of railing against vote by mail,

00:16:42.879 --> 00:16:47.129
when Donald Trump tweeted two days ago or
three days ago, everybody in Florida should

00:16:47.129 --> 00:16:48.769
vote by mail.

00:16:48.769 --> 00:16:53.639
My mind immediately goes to what does he know
about Ron DeSantis, his plans that now all

00:16:53.639 --> 00:16:57.570
of a sudden make him very into the idea of
Florida voting by mail.

00:16:57.570 --> 00:17:00.879
And I think it goes along the lines you're
talking about, which is the role that the

00:17:00.879 --> 00:17:04.000
state and secretaries of states may have.

00:17:04.000 --> 00:17:05.000
Yeah.

00:17:05.000 --> 00:17:12.770
I mean, Luckies doesn't even try to get principle
a any any do.

00:17:12.770 --> 00:17:17.309
I mean, he's basically saying, OK, in the
states that I need absentee voting, please

00:17:17.309 --> 00:17:20.309
vote absentee in the states where absentee
voting may hurt me.

00:17:20.309 --> 00:17:21.309
Don't do it.

00:17:21.309 --> 00:17:23.650
It's just raw power.

00:17:23.650 --> 00:17:28.580
But this is they understand that if it were
an election where which was clean, where everyone

00:17:28.580 --> 00:17:33.059
who should be wanting to should be voting
and wanting to vote votes, Trump can't overcome

00:17:33.059 --> 00:17:34.059
those numbers.

00:17:34.059 --> 00:17:38.460
So the disenfranchisement, the tried trying
to create confusion.

00:17:38.460 --> 00:17:44.110
And then the biggest risk, I think, is this
idea that Donald Trump imagine up on election

00:17:44.110 --> 00:17:49.220
days, up by a couple of points in Wisconsin
or Florida, even if a week later we know he's

00:17:49.220 --> 00:17:51.130
going to lose by four or five points.

00:17:51.130 --> 00:17:53.559
You could see him declaring victory.

00:17:53.559 --> 00:17:58.940
And it becomes much harder then for the states
where those state legislators are in Trump

00:17:58.940 --> 00:18:01.220
heavy districts to do the right thing.

00:18:01.220 --> 00:18:06.070
And so that that, I think, is the bigger,
bigger fear of how they would cheat or take

00:18:06.070 --> 00:18:07.600
but take the election.

00:18:07.600 --> 00:18:08.780
I completely agree.

00:18:08.780 --> 00:18:11.470
And it's exactly the point that I've been
making.

00:18:11.470 --> 00:18:16.510
Congressman Roe Kanha, representing California's
17th District.

00:18:16.510 --> 00:18:18.760
Always a pleasure and I really appreciate
your time.

00:18:18.760 --> 00:18:20.040
Appreciate it.

00:18:20.040 --> 00:18:21.040
Appreciate all of your time.

00:18:21.040 --> 00:18:22.240
And look forward to being back on

